  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 123 AM CDT Sun Sep 27 2026\n\nHigh pressure is pumping in a dry airmass across the western Great\nLakes while an approaching low brings mid and high level clouds in\nacross the region. In areas without so many clouds, some fog is\nforming, mainly across northeast Wisconsin and the lakeshore. This\nfog should dissipate sometime this morning after sunrise and after\nsome mechanical mixing. The approaching low will bring a shortwave\nsome some better moisture in across western Wisconsin during the\nday. While most of this precipitation will stay to the west given\nthe aforementioned feed of dry air from a high over eastern\nCanada, some light rain or sprinkles could sneak their way in\nacross western portions of central and north-central Wisconsin\nduring the day.\n\nDry weather and light winds return tonight as the main energy from\nthe low lifts to the northeast. Models suggest fog will be a bit\nmore dense and ubiquitous tonight into Monday morning given\nabundant low level moisture, clearing skies, and light winds. The\nworst of the fog looks like it will be across north-central\nWisconsin, although it could also develop across central Wisconsin\nwest of Highway 39. Given current trends it would not be entirely\nshocking for a Dense Fog Advisory in the future late tonight into\nMonday morning, especially for north-central Wisconsin, if trends\ncontinue.\n\nA more widespread period of precipitation is expected from \nTuesday afternoon through Wednesday night. Models have taken a\nturn this run, with the chance for half an inch or more of rain\nincreasing to 40 to 60% and a 20 to 40 percent chance of \nreceiving one inch or more of rain. This trend is due to the\nslower progression of the low through the western Great Lakes,\ngiving the area more time to reach those rainfall amounts. We are\nstill only on Sunday, so further adjustments are still possible as\nthis system gets sampled by the RAOB network.\n\nDry weather trends favored Thursday through Saturday: Forecast \npredictability decreases considerably from Thursday onward due to\na large spread in guidance regarding the evolution of the \nsouthern stream system. Despite this uncertainty, current trends \nsupport a higher potential for dry weather returning to the area \nfrom Thursday through Saturday.",
